Functional references
=====================

The LZ4 block decompressor in rtl/ was written from the published block-format
description.  No code was copied from any of the works below; they are listed
because they shaped the design, supplied the test data, or are the comparison
this block is measured against.


1. LZ4 reference implementation - lib/lz4.c, lib/lz4.h, lib/lz4hc.c, lib/lz4hc.h
   Copyright (c) Yann Collet. All rights reserved.
   BSD 2-Clause License - https://opensource.org/licenses/bsd-license.php
   https://github.com/lz4/lz4

   Used as the normative definition of the block format and as the compressor
   that produced the test streams.  The streams under vectors/ were produced by
   linking the unmodified library (or the equivalent python-lz4 binding) and
   compressing our own data; no part of the library is present in this
   repository, and nothing here is derived from its code.


2. AMD/Xilinx Vitis Data Compression library - LZ4 kernels
   (Xilinx/Vitis_Libraries, data_compression/, tags v2022.1-v2024.1)
   Apache License 2.0

   Prior art and the resource comparison quoted in the README and in
   doc/lz4_hardware_survey.md (L1 lz4_decompress: 703 LUTs, 591 flip-flops,
   32 BRAM, 375 MHz on XCU200).  No code from it is present here, and the
   figures are quoted from AMD's published reports, not re-measured by us.


3. Test input not redistributed

   During development one vector was a 256 KiB slice of a Linux kernel `Image`
   (GPL-2.0) compressed with lz4hc.  It is not shipped here, because its
   decompressed form is kernel code and this drop is Apache-2.0.
   doc/lz4_decoder_resources.md records what it proved and
   scripts/gen_vectors.py can rebuild an equivalent one from any kernel Image.


Nothing in this repository is derived from the copyright holder of the LZ4
reference implementation beyond the format itself, which is an open
specification: this is an independent implementation of a published file format,
not a port.
